tools agent_message send --to agent-01M2K1RFG71SJETW3996TFKAT0 --message-stdin <<'EOF'
Research result (read-only, nothing built, nothing posted). Key finding first, because it likely explains everything.

KEY FINDING (verified from our own model files + ORT docs): the "fixed shapes" in the last build are NOT fixed as far as DirectML is concerned. I read the model inputs: det.onnx input x = [DynamicDimension.0, 3, DynamicDimension.1, DynamicDimension.2]; rec.onnx input x = [DynamicDimension.0, 3, 48, DynamicDimension.1]. All free dims. Our session builder never overrides them, so DirectML builds the session as fully dynamic and only my runtime inputs are constant. The ORT DirectML doc says: "works most efficiently when tensor shapes are known at the time a session is created" (constant folding, more init work at session creation instead of first run, weight preprocessing, graph optimisation inside DirectML), and for models with free dimensions you must either edit the model to fixed dim_value, or "Specify values of named dimensions ... AddFreeDimensionOverrideByName", or use denotations. Source: https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/execution-providers/DirectML-ExecutionProvider.html (Performance Tuning section). Users report the same symptom: dynamic-axis DML ran 250 ms vs 50 ms after removing the dynamic axis (https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime/issues/14550); "dynamic shape input is much slower than fixed shape input in gpu" (https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime/issues/6978); every changed input size slows inference (https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime/issues/15394). The ort 2.0.0-rc.10 crate we use has with_dimension_override(name, size) (in its source, impl_options.rs line 204), so this is a small change. Inference (not sourced): this fits his symptoms, slow per call plus long warm-up plus "bigger batch much faster" (per-call overhead dominates when each call runs the generic dynamic path).

Recommended plan, ranked by expected benefit for an unknown Windows GPU:
1. Real fixed-shape sessions via dimension overrides (DynamicDimension.0/.1 for rec, .0/.1/.2 for det). Sessions are shape-specific, so use few: 1 detector session (one canvas, e.g. 1024x1280 or the current 1152x1536, rotate landscape pages instead of a second canvas) and 2-3 recognizer sessions, e.g. widths 320 / 800 / 1280 each with a LARGE batch (32 for 320, 16 for 800, 8 for 1280), with the batch padded. That cuts calls per page from ~12 to ~3-4 and lets DirectML do its upfront optimisation. Honest risk (unsourced): each fixed session still has its own creation/compile cost, so total warm-up may not shrink; it should drop per-page time hugely, and creation could be done lazily (create a session only when a page needs that width). Unverified until his PC; I cannot run DML here.
2. Drop my with_memory_pattern(false)/parallel(false)? The DML doc says DML does not support memory pattern or parallel execution and they must be disabled (https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/execution-providers/DirectML-ExecutionProvider.html). One user reports they "don't seem to be required any more" and removing them helped (https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime/issues/20983, edit 10/06/24). Low-risk test, small expected gain; I would test it behind a timing run, not assume.
3. Keep optimisation level at the default (ORT_ENABLE_ALL is the documented default for DML). Nothing to gain.
4. Persisting compile results between runs: I found no supported DirectML compile cache. EP-context caching is documented for NPU backends (https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/execution-providers/EP-Context-Design.html); I found nothing saying DML supports it. Microsoft's Windows ML page says apps must cache compiled output themselves or users pay each start, but that is the new Windows ML API, not our ORT build (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/ai/new-windows-ml/model-compilation). The practical equivalent: keep one long-lived worker per OCR job (already done, recycle at 40 pages) and warm up lazily so pages with only common widths do not compile the 1280 shape. Likely the driver also caches some shaders; one third-party note says DML shader compile is "cached after first use" (https://github.com/ChharithOeun/onnxruntime-directml-setup), low-quality source.
5. CPU is probably NOT unusable. Sourced numbers (PaddleOCR docs, model inference only, no pre/post-processing, per image or per text line, on a standard Intel CPU test box; the page does not state cores): PP-OCRv4_mobile_det 56.6 ms standard mode / 20.8 ms high-performance mode; PP-OCRv4_mobile_rec 17.5 ms / 3.6 ms (https://www.paddleocr.ai/main/en/version3.x/module_usage/text_recognition.html, https://paddlepaddle.github.io/PaddleX/3.2/en/pipeline_usage/tutorials/ocr_pipelines/OCR.html; v2.9 benchmark with Xeon 6148 + MKL-DNN: https://www.paddleocr.ai/v2.9.1/en/ppocr/infer_deploy/benchmark.html). Rec is per line, so a 50-line page is roughly 0.2-0.9 s plus detection, in the tens-of-ms to sub-second range on a desktop CPU with oneDNN. Our own measured CPU (my sandbox, 2 threads under Wine, i.e. a weak proxy): 4.3-5 s per normal page with 417-455 words, 20 s for a 1.5k-word first page. A modern 8+ core desktop at 4-8x more threads would be roughly 1-2 s/page (inference, not measured). The old build's CPU path on his PC has not been timed, so please have him say or let me add a benchmark. His CPU worry is unproven either way.
6. Current CPU settings in the app: intra-op threads = std::thread::available_parallelism() (all logical cores), no inter-op threads set, default graph optimisation, CPU sessions fully dynamic and variable batch (no padding), one worker process. Fine; CPU sessions with dynamic shapes are not penalised the way DML is.
7. Alternatives, lower priority:
 - OpenVINO EP or oneDNN on CPU: would need a different ORT build and DLLs, adds size and complexity; only worthwhile on Intel. Not sourced for speedup claims here; skip unless CPU turns out slow.
 - int8 quantised rec/det: ORT supports dynamic quantisation (https://onnxruntime.ai/docs/performance/model-optimizations/quantization.html), but accuracy loss on OCR is a risk I have no sourced numbers for; would need our own accuracy test. Only for the CPU fallback, later.
 - Multi-threaded CPU preprocessing and render/GPU pipelining: our own inference, small gain, safe.
 - RapidOCR is the same ORT pipeline with the same models; it also exposes DML as optional "stricter requirements" (https://github.com/RapidAI/RapidOCR/pull/178), and has known slower detector post-processing than Paddle (https://github.com/RapidAI/RapidOCR/issues/514). No evidence it solves this for us. CUDA/TensorRT would need NVIDIA-only DLLs (hundreds of MB), not suitable for an unknown GPU.
 - Re-check anything shipped fast: since pages 4-15 averaged 10 s and his prior build did 0.5-0.9 s on normal pages, the old build was clearly using the dynamic path with warm shapes; the issue then is the compile/per-call cost of each NEW shape, which fixed override sessions turn into a one-time creation cost. That is inference.

Recommended build, if you say go: (a) per-stage timing lines in ocr.log (render, det prep, det run, rec prep, rec run with call count, decode) and a startup line with GPU adapter name and session creation time per shape; (b) fixed sessions via dimension overrides with 1 detector + 3 recognizer sessions and large batches, created lazily, no up-front warm-up of unused widths; (c) A/B env switch to test memory-pattern on/off in the same run if cheap; (d) a per-page speed watchdog: if GPU pages average slower than ~3 s after 3 pages, log it and offer CPU (CPU auto-switch only on error today); (e) a timed CPU benchmark line so we know his real CPU speed instead of guessing. All GPU benefits remain unverified until he runs it. Under Wine I can only validate correctness of the fixed-shape code on CPU (the override works on any provider), not DML speed.
EOF
